## Method and reasoning
Sort by finish time. For each screening find how many earlier screenings end at or before its start. Taking this screening combines its reward with the optimum on that compatible prefix. Skipping it keeps the preceding optimum. Use upper_bound so touching screenings are allowed.

## Walk through the sample
Taking [2,6) for 15 and [6,7) for 2 yields 17. Taking the two earlier compatible screenings gives only 6 + 4 before the last one. An end exactly equal to the next start is compatible.

## Complexity
Time O(n log n), space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct Screening {
    int start, end, reward;
};
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<Screening> a(n);
    for (auto &item : a)
        cin >> item.start >> item.end >> item.reward;
    sort(a.begin(), a.end(), [](auto x, auto y) { return x.end < y.end; });
    vector<int> ends;
    for (auto item : a)
        ends.push_back(item.end);
    vector<long long> best(n + 1);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int p = upper_bound(ends.begin(), ends.begin() + i, a[i].start) - ends.begin();
        best[i + 1] = max(best[i], best[p] + a[i].reward);
    }
    cout << best[n] << '\n';
}
~~~
